.SH NAME
dup, dup2 \- duplicate an open file descriptor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B int dup
.I (fildes)
.br
.B int
.I fildes;
.br
.B int dup2
.I (fildes, fildes2)
.br
.B int
.I fildes, fildes2;
.SH DESCRIPTION
Given
a file descriptor returned from
an
.BR open (2),
.BR pipe (2),
or
.BR creat (2)
call,
.B dup
allocates another file descriptor
synonymous
with
the original.
The new file descriptor is returned.
It has 
the following in common with the original:
.IP
Same open file (or pipe).
.IP
Same file pointer.
(i.e., both file descriptors share one file pointer.)
.IP
Same access mode (read, write or read/write).
.PP
The new
file descriptor
is set to remain open across
.BR exec (2)
system calls.
See
.BR fcntl (2).
.PP
The
file descriptor
returned is the lowest one available.
.PP
In the second form of the call,
.I fildes
is a file descriptor referring to an open file, and
.I fildes2
is a non-negative integer less than the maximum value
allowed for file descriptors.
.B Dup2
causes
.I fildes2
to refer to the same file as
.I fildes.
If
.I fildes2
already referred to an open file, it is closed first.
.SH SEE ALSO
creat(2), close(2), exec(2), fcntl(2), open(2), pipe(2).
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B Dup
will fail if one or more of the following are true:
.IP
.I Fildes
is not a valid open
file descriptor.
.SM
\%[EBADF]
.IP
Forty (40)
file descriptors
are currently open.
.SM
\%[EMFILE]
.SH RETURN VALUE
Upon successful completion a non-negative integer,
namely the
file descriptor,
is returned.
Otherwise, a value of \-1 is returned and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.SH ASSEMBLER
.nf
.na
    CONSTANT DUP	:= 41

    ...		!* fildes, fildes2 in r0, r1 respectively	*!
    set	r0,#6	!* dup2 calls only				*!
    sc	#DUP
    ...		!* return value in r4  *!
       		!* carry flag set if error *!
.fi
.ad
.PP
The
.B dup2
entry is implemented by in an OR condition 0100 with
.I fildes.
